{
    "word": "jackanapes",
    "lang_code": "en",
    "source_file_to_entries": {
        "raw-wiktextract-data.jsonl": [
            {
                "entry_id": "85f32e96-d086-32bf-b50e-36316e1adaae",
                "word": "jackanapes",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "83173a44-1e20-4816-8a57-aed3418fd227",
                        "entry_id": "85f32e96-d086-32bf-b50e-36316e1adaae",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "jackanapeses"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "c17299d7-ac02-38bf-9c46-dc0672475c5d",
                        "entry_id": "85f32e96-d086-32bf-b50e-36316e1adaae",
                        "glosses": [
                            "(A proper name for) an ape or monkey, especially a tame one kept for entertainment or as a pet."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "obsolete"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "33abcf42-92cc-43cf-824e-b8b3d6b5698b",
                                "sense_id": "c17299d7-ac02-38bf-9c46-dc0672475c5d",
                                "text": "To iettynge, to iaggynge, and to full of iapes; / To mockynge, to mowynge, to lye a iackenapes: […]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "97142575-2986-44b1-9885-8cc50073f9fe",
                                "sense_id": "c17299d7-ac02-38bf-9c46-dc0672475c5d",
                                "text": "He grynnes and he gapis, / As it were iack napis."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "19052523-c803-4dff-91d6-cd12c58334b5",
                                "sense_id": "c17299d7-ac02-38bf-9c46-dc0672475c5d",
                                "text": "Can Iack an Ape be merry vvhen his clog is at his heele."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "a7856c6b-9894-466b-afce-cd8c8c67e7cf",
                                "sense_id": "c17299d7-ac02-38bf-9c46-dc0672475c5d",
                                "text": "A fair sight we are; and had I but a rebeck or guitar at my back, and a jackanapes on my shoulder, we should seem as joyous a brace of strollers as ever touched string at a castle gate."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "55840c8c-bb97-4ad1-9d54-48d592301fc1",
                                "sense_id": "c17299d7-ac02-38bf-9c46-dc0672475c5d",
                                "text": "[…] I could see him climbing like a jackanapes, for that part was again very steep; […]"
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "be94d316-b5ca-32ad-b41b-bf3f52458688",
                        "entry_id": "85f32e96-d086-32bf-b50e-36316e1adaae",
                        "glosses": [
                            "(A proper name for) a person thought to behave like an ape or monkey, for example, in being impudent, mischievous, vain, etc.; specifically (chiefly humorous), an impudent or mischievous child."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "broadly",
                            "dated",
                            "derogatory"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "2f3951ea-a1b5-43ab-ae59-2245ef4acd44",
                                "sense_id": "be94d316-b5ca-32ad-b41b-bf3f52458688",
                                "text": "I [Aye], quoth Jack a napes, by these ten bones, / Nothing happens amiss to a præparid minde, / Tis good philosophy, katt will to kinde."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "15188d63-bd9a-4a4b-8850-0efd9990d7bc",
                                "sense_id": "be94d316-b5ca-32ad-b41b-bf3f52458688",
                                "text": "VVas there euer man had ſuch lucke? vvhen I kiſt the Iacke vpon an vp-caſt, to be hit avvay? I had a hundred pound on't: and then a vvhoreſon Iacke-an-apes muſt take me vp for ſvvearing, as if I borrovved mine oathes of him, and might not ſpend them at my pleaſure."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "d42433e1-f170-402d-80de-da999365cd5d",
                                "sense_id": "be94d316-b5ca-32ad-b41b-bf3f52458688",
                                "text": "Now, she hath favoured, doth favour, and will favour, this jack-an-ape,—for what good part about him I know not, save that as one noble lady will love a messan dog, and another a screaming popinjay, and a third a Barbary ape, so doth it please our noble dame to set her affections upon this stray elf of a page, […]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "3b15f76c-787f-4dea-b7f1-c8616d96ee8a",
                                "sense_id": "be94d316-b5ca-32ad-b41b-bf3f52458688",
                                "text": "I beg to say, that I use those last expressions advisedly, sir, and not in the sense in which they are now used by Jackanapeses. There were no Jackanapeses when I was a boy, Mr. Hood. England was Old England when I was young."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "c866fe2e-1c30-4d30-88df-a59b69ef231a",
                                "sense_id": "be94d316-b5ca-32ad-b41b-bf3f52458688",
                                "text": "[W]hat right has any free, reasonable soul on earth, to sell himself for a shilling a-day to murder any man, right or wrong— […] just because such a whiskered, profligate jackanapes as that officer, […] is set to command grey-headed men before he can command his own meanest passions."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "23ade9b0-0830-4095-9405-bf6c508d3c98",
                                "sense_id": "be94d316-b5ca-32ad-b41b-bf3f52458688",
                                "text": "Then I set the boy down as a conceited young jackanapes, which no doubt he was,—but so are a great many other young people of Ernest's age."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "a8b36025-bf8f-4122-b853-46c23819d799",
                                "sense_id": "be94d316-b5ca-32ad-b41b-bf3f52458688",
                                "text": "[…] I'd soon make you laugh the other side of your mouth, you guffawing jackanapes."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "6ca64ae1-016b-48b8-8a24-5c31c336dd4c",
                                "sense_id": "be94d316-b5ca-32ad-b41b-bf3f52458688",
                                "text": "The boy wouldn't dare. (Rising choler.) Wouldn't dare to refuse the only sensible girl for miles around. That stubborn jackanapes refuse to marry a girl like you!"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "dda1e0b7-d29b-44c4-a68b-1364e9c97778",
                                "sense_id": "be94d316-b5ca-32ad-b41b-bf3f52458688",
                                "text": "Who were the aristocracy, to give themselves airs? Jackanapes!"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "5980b17d-38ff-4c50-9536-630d40ef99dc",
                                "sense_id": "be94d316-b5ca-32ad-b41b-bf3f52458688",
                                "text": "By his extreme youth, he can only be a prancing jackanapes, and so I name him."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "bfdd3357-bcc7-3f81-964a-46ec281ac398",
                        "entry_id": "85f32e96-d086-32bf-b50e-36316e1adaae",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A crucifix."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "broadly",
                            "derogatory",
                            "obsolete",
                            "rare"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "3d01ea0d-1cc1-48f2-b25b-f52e9f8b3a2f",
                                "sense_id": "bfdd3357-bcc7-3f81-964a-46ec281ac398",
                                "text": "I wyl rather haue these knees pared of, then I wil kneele to yonder iacknapes (meaning the rode) God healpe me I am borne to trouble and aduersity in this world."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "434e30ca-0fd6-3bd6-8253-b45a5f01c8d5",
                        "entry_id": "85f32e96-d086-32bf-b50e-36316e1adaae",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A small pulley which keeps a rope in line when lifting ore, water, etc., from a mine."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "broadly",
                            "obsolete"
                        ],
                        "examples": []
                    }
